Q: Do collection expressions like [] always create a new object? A: <p>In addition to the previous answer several notes:</p>
<h6>1.</h6>
<blockquote>
<p>And what in the case where I have fixed values like that:</p>
<pre><code>int[] GetCollection() =&gt; [100, 200];
</code></pre>
<p>I understand that in case I would return a mutable collection</p>
</blockquote>
<p>in case of <code>int[]</code> the collection is still mutable, i.e. you can do <code>GetCollection()[0] = 42;</code>, so new instance is obviously required.</p>
<h6>2.</h6>
<p>Check out the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/proposals/csharp-12.0/collection-expressions#empty-collection-literal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">feature specification</a> which can give some more insights about what can happen. For example for empty collection literal:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The empty collection expression is permitted to be a singleton if used to construct a final collection value that is known to not be mutable:</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Can be a singleton, like Array.Empty&lt;int&gt;()
int[] x = []; 

// Can be a singleton. Allowed to use Array.Empty&lt;int&gt;(), Enumerable.Empty&lt;int&gt;(),
// or any other implementation that can not be mutated.
IEnumerable&lt;int&gt; y = [];

// Must not be a singleton.  Value must be allowed to mutate, and should not mutate
// other references elsewhere.
List&lt;int&gt; z = [];
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>Note the <em>&quot;is permitted&quot;</em> part, so for your first case it is not required to be (but will be a singleton and taking in account all the performance-centric work done in the runtime lately - very-very unlikely that it will ever change)</p>
